      "locked": false,
      "text": "                       +-----------------------------------------------+\n                       |           ASSEMBLER DESIGN VARIANTS           |\n                       +-----------------------+-----------------------+\n                                               |\n       +---------------------------------------+-----------------------+\n       |                                       |                       |\n       v                                       v                       v\n+------------------------------+------------------------------+------------------------------+\n|  1. IC FORMAT VARIANTS       |  2. EXECUTION MODE VARIANTS  |  3. PASS COUNT VARIANTS      |\n|  * Variant I (Compact IC)    |  * Load-and-Go (In-RAM)     |  * Single-Pass (Fast)      |\n|  * Variant II (Balanced IC)  |  * Object File (Target File) |  * Two-Pass (Standard)       |\n|                              |                              |  * Multi-Pass (Chained EQU) |\n+------------------------------+------------------------------+------------------------------+\n\n=============================================================================================\n                          SUMMARY MATRIX OF DESIGN VARIANTS\n=============================================================================================\n| Dimension      | Variant Name | Key Characteristic         | Main Advantage         | Main Disadvantage      |\n+----------------+--------------+----------------------------+------------------------+------------------------+\n| Intermediate   | Variant I    | Converts memory operands   | Highly compact IC;     | Heavy Pass 1 workload; |\n| Code           |              | to (S, #n) tuples in Pass 1| Pass 2 is fast         | unbalanced memory size |\n+----------------+--------------+----------------------------+------------------------+------------------------+\n| Intermediate   | Variant II   | Keeps memory operands as   | Balances workload      | IC file size is larger |\n| Code           |              | raw source strings in IC   | between passes; good   | and less compact       |\n|                |              |                            | for expressions (A+5)  |                        |\n+----------------+--------------+----------------------------+------------------------+------------------------+\n| Execution Mode | Load-and-Go  | Assembles target code      | Fast build-and-run     | Program cannot be      |\n|                |              | directly into RAM          | cycle; no disk I/O     | saved as an executable |\n+----------------+--------------+----------------------------+------------------------+------------------------+\n| Execution Mode | Object       | Outputs target code to a   | Creates permanent,     | Requires disk file     |\n|                | Module       | .obj / .o file             | reusable binary files  | I/O overhead           |\n+----------------+--------------+----------------------------+------------------------+------------------------+",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "baseFontSize": null,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "                       +-----------------------------------------------+\n                       |           ASSEMBLER DESIGN VARIANTS           |\n                       +-----------------------+-----------------------+\n                                               |\n       +---------------------------------------+-----------------------+\n       |                                       |                       |\n       v                                       v                       v\n+------------------------------+------------------------------+------------------------------+\n|  1. IC FORMAT VARIANTS       |  2. EXECUTION MODE VARIANTS  |  3. PASS COUNT VARIANTS      |\n|  * Variant I (Compact IC)    |  * Load-and-Go (In-RAM)     |  * Single-Pass (Fast)      |\n|  * Variant II (Balanced IC)  |  * Object File (Target File) |  * Two-Pass (Standard)       |\n|                              |                              |  * Multi-Pass (Chained EQU) |\n+------------------------------+------------------------------+------------------------------+\n\n=============================================================================================\n                          SUMMARY MATRIX OF DESIGN VARIANTS\n=============================================================================================\n| Dimension      | Variant Name | Key Characteristic         | Main Advantage         | Main Disadvantage      |\n+----------------+--------------+----------------------------+------------------------+------------------------+\n| Intermediate   | Variant I    | Converts memory operands   | Highly compact IC;     | Heavy Pass 1 workload; |\n| Code           |              | to (S, #n) tuples in Pass 1| Pass 2 is fast         | unbalanced memory size |\n+----------------+--------------+----------------------------+------------------------+------------------------+\n| Intermediate   | Variant II   | Keeps memory operands as   | Balances workload      | IC file size is larger |\n| Code           |              | raw source strings in IC   | between passes; good   | and less compact       |\n|                |              |                            | for expressions (A+5)  |                        |\n+----------------+--------------+----------------------------+------------------------+------------------------+\n| Execution Mode | Load-and-Go  | Assembles target code      | Fast build-and-run     | Program cannot be      |\n|                |              | directly into RAM          | cycle; no disk I/O     | saved as an executable |\n+----------------+--------------+----------------------------+------------------------+------------------------+\n| Execution Mode | Object       | Outputs target code to a   | Creates permanent,     | Requires disk file     |\n|                | Module       | .obj / .o file             | reusable binary files  | I/O overhead           |\n+----------------+--------------+----------------------------+------------------------+------------------------+",
